invariant: INV-17
file: p256_scalar.c
catches: proof/prove-one.sh p256_scalar
reason: the conditional subtraction of n returns without wiping its
  difference, which is its operand less n modulo 2^256 and so gives the
  operand to whoever reads it. p256_scalar_add hands it z + r*d. The
  array's lifetime ends with the call, so no vector reads it, and whether
  a limb of it stays on the stack depends on the compiler: under gcc 13.3
  at -O1 for x86-64 one did, and at -O2 none.
  proof/p256_scalar_harness.c counts the bytes ct_wipe is handed and
  requires one array's worth after p256_scalar_reduce and two after
  p256_scalar_add. Each run is a proof, so this belongs to the nightly's
  test-invariants-proof-backed job.

--- old
    select_limbs(o, reduced, t, ~(uint32_t)(below >> 32));
    ct_wipe(reduced, sizeof reduced);
--- new
    select_limbs(o, reduced, t, ~(uint32_t)(below >> 32));
